  "refuter_findings": [
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:3:1",
      "id": "lem-coefficient-field-purely-inseparable-complete-step",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Statement claims that a residue-injective subfield K is contained in a coefficient field whenever k/rho(K) is purely inseparable. Let A=F_p(s)[[t]], m=(t), v=s^p+t, and K=F_p(v). Then K is residue-injective with rho(K)=F_p(s^p), while k=F_p(s) is purely inseparable over rho(K). If a coefficient field C contained K, its lift c in C of s would satisfy c^p=v, since c^p and v are both in C and have residue s^p. But every pth power in F_p(s)[[t]] lies in F_p(s^p)[[t^p]], so it has no t-term and cannot equal s^p+t. Thus no such C exists.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "3c5b173a9e736f9102f6b0923a4b7274410d87f934a4938ee7506f62abdccb5a"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:3:2",
      "id": "cor-equicharacteristic-complete-local-ring-has-coefficient-field",
      "location": "3.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 3.1 says, “Then [L3] shows that K is contained in a coefficient field of A. Maximality forces that coefficient field to equal K.” Its cited L3 is the false purely-inseparable lemma. In A=F_p(s)[[t]], extend F_p(s^p+t) to a maximal residue-injective subfield. Its residue image cannot contain s, since that would give a pth root of s^p+t in A; hence its residue image is the proper field F_p(s^p). The invocation of L3 therefore does not license the conclusion that rho(K)=k.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "71858a31f2e20d365701e138dd2051bb20856badfa1042113733e3ca6935d968"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:3:3",
      "id": "lem-cohen-presentation-surjective-by-completeness",
      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The Statement asserts surjectivity for every continuous k-algebra map that is surjective modulo every m^n. Take k=F_p[t], A=F_p[[t]], m=(t), and phi:k[[X]] -> A defined by t maps to t and X maps to 0. This map is continuous, and each map to A/(t^n) is surjective because F_p[t] -> F_p[[t]]/(t^n) is surjective. But im(phi)=F_p[t], which omits 1+t+t^2+..., so phi is not surjective.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "0b472a8ad9a297894449268e9760f860420c08c771149a26d0a14f9912174693"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:3:4",
      "id": "ex-hensel-factor-correction-mod-three-powers",
      "location": "1.1",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 displays “1=(-1)·T+1·(T-1) mod t”. The right-hand side is -T+T-1=-1, not 1 (for example over k=Q). Thus the asserted Bezout computation is false under the stated unrestricted field hypothesis.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "9482258ad6362222b801b455cb58e1100a90af914e6c577c37bb835ad466b5bd"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:3:5",
      "id": "ex-idempotent-lifting-and-product-decomposition",
      "location": "2.1",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "[L1] states only that idempotents lift uniquely “in a Henselian pair,” but Step 2.1 applies it to the given “complete pair” without establishing or citing that (A,I) is Henselian. The claim can be closed by verifying I-adic completeness/separatedness and invoking cor-complete-separated-adic-pair-henselian.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "0bdd435ffb469425cbc990320209760c47011c11e65286a099c3f1569704ba15"
